BOB-166 — closure-migration guard: RUNTIME evidence (§11.4.226 runtime class)
captured: 2026-08-25T20:18:27Z

WHY THIS COULD NOT CLOSE EARLIER. Ten rows carried a terminal status while still
located in 'Issues'. Moving them was a STATE-ONLY repair, and §11.4.226(5) is
explicit that a state repair cannot close a defect as fixed — nothing stopped the
eleventh row. The mechanism was missing, so the item stayed open. Correctly.

WHAT WAS ACTUALLY WRONG — and it was not what it looked like. The guard already
existed. Constitution commit 71589d5 ("refuse a terminal status that skips its own
migration") is an ancestor of the current constitution HEAD and has been for some
time. The guard was in the SOURCE the whole while.

It was not in the BINARY. pre_build invariant 17 resolves through a candidate loop
whose first entry is the git-TRACKED bin/workable-items, and that binary was six
days stale (BOB-188). Measured then and now:

  "Issues-location item has TERMINAL status"   in source: 1   in shipped binary: 0
  after the BOB-188 rebuild                    in source: 1   in shipped binary: 1

So BOB-166's fix was inert for exactly as long as the shipped artifact lagged its
source. The ten rows did not survive a missing guard; they survived a guard that
could not run. BOB-188 is what made BOB-166 operative — this is a §11.4.108
SOURCE->ARTIFACT gap wearing a tracker defect's clothes.

--- RUNTIME PROBE (on a COPY of the DB, never the live one) ---
victim: BOB-065, status=Queued, current_location=Issues

GOLDEN-TRUE — set a terminal status without migrating:
  $ workable-items update --id BOB-065 --status "Fixed (→ Fixed.md)"
  exit=1
  update: refusing to set terminal status "Fixed (→ Fixed.md)" on BOB-065 while it
  is located in Issues — a terminal status without the migration strands a closed
  item in the OPEN tracker (§11.4.19 requires the closure move to be atomic;
  §11.4.15/ATM-627 INTEG-03).
    new closure (records captured evidence):  close ... --evidence <path>
    already closed elsewhere (reconcile):     move --id ... --to Fixed --why <text>
  row after refusal: ('BOB-065', 'Queued', 'Issues')   <- did NOT move

GOLDEN-FALSE — §11.4.201(1) false-positive guard, a legitimate change:
  $ workable-items update --id BOB-065 --status "In progress"
  exit=0   update: BOB-065 updated in Issues (status=In progress, type=Task)

Both directions hold: it bites the violation and stays silent on lawful use. A
guard that only ever refused would be a FAIL-bluff of equal severity.

--- STATE ---
terminal-status rows still located in 'Issues': 0

--- HONEST BOUNDARY (§11.4.6) ---
This proves the guard refuses at the update seam of THIS binary. It does not prove
every write path is covered — a direct SQL write bypasses it entirely, which is why
§11.4.146(D3) mandates a full-table SWEEP gate as the second seam rather than
trusting the engine alone. That sweep's presence is not asserted here.
